# Safe TypeScript Error Handling & `any` Usage Standard
Version: 1.0
Status: Mandatory Platform Architecture Rule

## Purpose

This standard prevents platform crashes caused by:

- `any` typed values
- `unknown` errors
- failed API responses
- failed database calls
- failed Edge Functions
- invalid JSON
- unexpected null values
- runtime exceptions
- uncaught Promise rejections

No component, hook, page, provider, service, Edge Function, or utility may be capable of crashing the application because of an improperly handled error or unsafe type.

---

# Core Rule

Platform availability is more important than feature availability.

If a feature fails:

- the feature fails safely
- the page continues rendering
- the application continues functioning
- the user receives an appropriate notification
- the error is logged
- the platform never crashes

---

# Rule 1 — Never Trust `any`

Avoid:

```ts
const result = response as any;
```

Instead:

```ts
const result = response as Partial<MyResponse>;
```

or

```ts
const result = response as unknown;
```

Validate before use.

Example

```ts
if (
    result &&
    typeof result === "object" &&
    "status" in result
) {

}
```

---

# Rule 2 — Never Assume Error Shape

Avoid

```ts
catch (error: any) {
    toast.error(error.message);
}
```

Use

```ts
catch (error: unknown) {

    console.error(error);

    const message =
        error instanceof Error
            ? error.message
            : "Unexpected error";

    toast.error(message);

}
```

---

# Rule 3 — Every Catch Must Return Safe State

Every catch block must restore application stability.

Example

```ts
catch (error) {

    console.error(error);

    setLoading(false);

    setData([]);

    toast.error("Unable to load data");

}
```

Never leave

- loading=true
- submitting=true
- disabled=true

after failure.

---

# Rule 4 — Never Throw During Rendering

Forbidden

```tsx
const value = data.items[0].name;
```

Safe

```tsx
const value =
    data?.items?.[0]?.name ??
    "Unavailable";
```

Rendering must never throw.

---

# Rule 5 — Never Crash From Missing Data

Always provide defaults.

Example

```ts
const users = response?.users ?? [];
```

```ts
const total = response?.total ?? 0;
```

```ts
const name = profile?.name ?? "";
```

---

# Rule 6 — Validate External Data

Everything received from

- Supabase
- REST APIs
- Edge Functions
- Browser APIs
- Local Storage
- Session Storage
- JSON
- URL Parameters

must be validated.

Never assume structure.

---

# Rule 7 — Promise Safety

Never write

```ts
loadData();
```

Write

```ts
void loadData().catch(console.error);
```

or

```ts
await loadData();
```

inside try/catch.

No Promise rejection may remain unhandled.

---

# Rule 8 — Safe Async Effects

Every async effect must support cancellation.

Example

```ts
useEffect(() => {

    let mounted = true;

    async function load(){

        try{

            ...

            if(!mounted) return;

            setData(data);

        }

        catch(error){

            console.error(error);

        }

    }

    void load();

    return () => {

        mounted = false;

    };

}, []);
```

---

# Rule 9 — Never Assume Database Success

Always check

```ts
const { data, error } =
await supabase...

if(error){

    throw error;

}
```

Never access

```ts
data.users
```

without verifying

```ts
data
```

exists.

---

# Rule 10 — Safe Array Access

Never

```ts
users[0]
```

Use

```ts
users?.[0]
```

---

# Rule 11 — Safe Object Access

Never

```ts
user.profile.name
```

Use

```ts
user?.profile?.name
```

---

# Rule 12 — Safe Date Handling

Never

```ts
format(new Date(date))
```

Use

```ts
const parsed =
new Date(date);

if(!isNaN(parsed.getTime())){

    format(parsed);

}
```

---

# Rule 13 — Safe JSON Parsing

Never

```ts
JSON.parse(value)
```

without protection.

Always

```ts
try{

    return JSON.parse(value);

}

catch{

    return {};
}
```

---

# Rule 14 — Safe Storage Access

Never assume

```ts
localStorage
```

exists.

Check

```ts
if(typeof window !== "undefined")
```

before use.

---

# Rule 15 — Safe Component Rendering

A component must never throw.

If required data is unavailable

display

- Skeleton
- Loading State
- Empty State
- Error State

instead of throwing.

---

# Rule 16 — Global Error Boundary

All pages must be protected by

```
<AppErrorBoundary>
```

Component failures must never terminate the application.

---

# Rule 17 — React Query

Every query

must include

- retry policy
- staleTime
- error state
- loading state

Never assume

```ts
data
```

exists.

---

# Rule 18 — Logging

Every unexpected error must be logged.

Minimum

```ts
console.error(error);
```

Preferred

Central logging service.

---

# Rule 19 — Feature Isolation

Failure of

- Payments

- Messaging

- Notifications

- Maps

- GPS

- Telemetry

- Analytics

- Reports

must never stop

- authentication

- routing

- dashboards

- navigation

- platform startup

---

# Rule 20 — Startup Protection

During application initialization

every provider

must fail independently.

One provider

must never stop

another provider.

---

# Rule 21 — Forbidden Patterns

Never use

```ts
catch(error:any)
```

Never use

```ts
throw error;
```

inside rendering.

Never access

```ts
foo.bar.baz
```

without validation.

Never ignore Promise rejections.

Never leave loading states active.

Never trust API responses.

Never trust database responses.

Never trust browser APIs.

---

# Mandatory Compliance

Every pull request must verify:

- No uncaught exceptions
- No unsafe `any`
- No unvalidated API response
- No unsafe property access
- No rendering exceptions
- No infinite loading states
- No unhandled Promise rejection
- No provider capable of crashing application startup
- Every catch restores a valid UI state
- Every error is logged
- Every feature degrades gracefully

---

# Platform Stability Guarantee

The platform architecture must always favor graceful degradation over application failure.

If any feature encounters an error, the feature shall fail safely, provide appropriate user feedback, log diagnostic information, restore a stable application state, and allow the remainder of the platform to continue operating without interruption. Under no circumstances shall an unhandled `any`, uncaught exception, rejected Promise, or malformed external data be permitted to terminate rendering, prevent application startup, or cause a platform-wide crash.
